window.PREMIERE_GUIDES = [{"id":"01-EDITING-GEOMETRY","title":"Editing geometry: source time, sequence time and four trims","filename":"guides/01-EDITING-GEOMETRY.md","html":"\u003ch1>Field guide 01 — Editing geometry: source time, sequence time and four trims\u003c/h1>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Read after:\u003c/strong> L016–L025. \u003cstrong>Goal:\u003c/strong> Predict an edit before touching the mouse.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Three coordinate systems\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>source file\u003c/strong> contains frames. Its frame 250 is frame 250 regardless of which sequence uses it. A \u003cstrong>source selection\u003c/strong> is a pair of boundaries, In and Out. A \u003cstrong>sequence\u003c/strong> is a new time axis containing instances that reference parts of source media. The \u003cstrong>playhead\u003c/strong> addresses sequence time; the Source Monitor playhead addresses source time. One source may appear in a sequence ten times without ten copies of the underlying file.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>For simple constant-speed clips with matching rates, the mapping is:\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>source frame = source in + (sequence frame - instance start)\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>For example, if B uses source frames 50–99 and starts at sequence frame 100, the viewer sees B source frame 62 at sequence frame 112. The camera file has not changed. At 25 fps a 50-frame selection lasts 2 seconds. Speed effects, reverse motion, remapping and mismatched frame rates change this mapping; do not apply the simple formula blindly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>A shared example\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Start with \u003ccode>A[0–99] | B[50–149] | C[0–99]\u003c/code>, all 25 fps. Three segments use 100 frames each, a 300-frame sequence (12 seconds). The notation is inclusive for explanation only: Premiere In/Out semantics and edit points should be checked with the frame display when executing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>Operation\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>What changes\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>What is deliberately preserved\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Predictable consequence\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Ripple trim\u003c/strong> B's left edge 25 frames later\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>B loses its first 25 used source frames; following clips shift earlier\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Earlier A untouched; remaining C source range stays the same\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Sequence becomes 25 frames shorter if no locks or other constraints stop the ripple\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Rolling edit\u003c/strong> A/B boundary 25 frames later\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>A extends 25 source frames and B starts 25 source frames later\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Combined A+B timeline duration; positions of material after B\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Requires handles; whole sequence duration stays constant\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Slip\u003c/strong> B's selected source range 25 frames later\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>B uses \u003ccode>[75–174]\u003c/code> instead of \u003ccode>[50–149]\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>B's timeline position, both edges, overall duration, A and C\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Content within B changes without moving its timeline boundaries\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Slide\u003c/strong> B 25 frames later\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>B's sequence position moves later and adjacent A/C boundaries compensate\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>B's source In/Out and duration; overall sequence duration\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Requires usable adjacent handles; A and C durations change\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>When performing a roll or slide, available source handles may limit how far you can move. Track locks, sync locks, linked selection, multi-track compositions, and transition handles change what is legally editable. \u003cstrong>Undo and inspect the full sequence\u003c/strong>, not just the active clip.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Insert versus overwrite\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Given \u003ccode>A | B\u003c/code> and an incoming two-second C:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Insert\u003c/strong> between A and B: \u003ccode>A | C | B\u003c/code>; eligible following material shifts by two seconds. Sequence grows by two seconds.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Overwrite\u003c/strong> at B's first frame: \u003ccode>A | C | ...B\u003c/code>; incoming frames replace the corresponding destination interval. If B has enough duration, total sequence duration need not change.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Source patching\u003c/strong> chooses which incoming source streams are mapped to timeline destinations (for example incoming video to V2, audio to A1). \u003cstrong>Track targeting\u003c/strong> influences many sequence commands and navigation; targeting a track does not automatically replace source patching. Check the timeline header controls before inserting. Both types of control can be customized.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>J-cuts and L-cuts without mystique\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A J-cut starts the incoming shot's audio before its picture. An L-cut keeps the outgoing shot's audio playing after its picture changes. They're not special video effects; they're intentional differences in audio and picture edit positions. Separate or unlink only the appropriate media, preserve synchronization reference, and listen across the boundary. A one-second lead-in is not automatically better than an eight-frame lead-in: editorial context decides.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Practice drill\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Duplicate a short practice sequence so the original can be restored.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Build three five-second clips A, B and C on V1 (and associated audio if useful).\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Record the original sequence end time and B's source In/Out.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ripple the end of A one second shorter; record the new sequence end and position of C.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Undo. Roll the A/B boundary one second later; check sequence duration and A/B source ranges.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Undo. Slip B one second later; check its timeline boundaries and source range.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Undo. Slide B one second later; check A/C durations and B's source range.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Explain every difference without saying only which button you clicked.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Mastery:\u003c/strong> You can predict total sequence duration, clip position, and source-frame choices for all four edits, including the effect of missing handles or track locks. See L018, L021–L025 and the diagram controls in the corresponding lessons.\u003c/p>\n","description":"Field guide 01 — Editing geometry: source time, sequence time and four trims Read after: L016–L025. Goal: Predict an edit before touching the mouse. Three coordinate systems A source file contains frames. Its frame 250 i"},{"id":"02-AUDIO-ENGINEERING","title":"Audio levels, loudness, repair and delivery","filename":"guides/02-AUDIO-ENGINEERING.md","html":"\u003ch1>Field guide 02 — Audio levels, loudness, repair and delivery\u003c/h1>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Read after:\u003c/strong> L038–L046. \u003cstrong>Goal:\u003c/strong> Understand the signal path, not memorize a target number.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Audio as a sequence of stages\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Audio arrives as samples in a clip, then passes through applicable clip-level controls/effects, track-level processing, routing and output mixing. \u003cstrong>Clip gain\u003c/strong> changes the signal before later volume and some downstream processing; \u003cstrong>volume automation\u003c/strong> is a time-varying level adjustment. Different places in the path give compressors and noise gates different signal levels, so the order can affect the outcome.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A good working order for dialogue is: select the right take → synchronize → remove clicks and edit boundaries → adjust gain and large level differences → cautious noise/speech repair → high-pass/EQ when justified → compression if needed → keyframe or automate phrases → mix against music/effects → meter → listen to the final exported file.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>dB, peaks and loudness\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Decibels express \u003cstrong>ratios\u003c/strong>, not an absolute sound level by themselves. For amplitude:\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>change in dB = 20 × log10(new amplitude / original amplitude)\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A +6 dB change approximately doubles amplitude; −6 dB approximately halves it. In digital audio, \u003cstrong>0 dBFS\u003c/strong> is the nominal digital sample full-scale ceiling. A track can distort upstream of the master even when the final meter looks lower, so review gain staging throughout the chain. A limiter may control output peaks but cannot necessarily undo existing clipping.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Peak level\u003c/strong> tracks brief extremes. \u003cstrong>Integrated loudness\u003c/strong> in LUFS estimates perceived loudness over an entire programme using a standardized weighting and gating method. Loudness targets vary by platform, broadcaster, delivery agreement and content; there is no universal “make every export −14 LUFS” requirement. If a client specifies true-peak dBTP and integrated LUFS, verify both using an appropriate loudness tool and report actual measured values. Sample peaks do not guarantee intersample true peaks are safe.\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>Symptom\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Likely cause\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Better first test\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Voice buried under music\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Music competing in same frequencies or level\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Lower music during speech; compare at normal volume\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Hiss becomes more obvious after compression\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Noise floor was raised\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Bypass compressor; address source noise before adding gain\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Pumping or unnatural speech\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Excessive repair/fast compression\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Reduce processing; compare unprocessed source\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Export clips despite a quiet timeline\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Gain or loudness changes in output path; peak overs\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Compare output file with meters; inspect output processing\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Channels sound wrong\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Mono/stereo mapping or unexpected channel assignment\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Inspect Modify &gt; Audio Channels and sequence routing\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\u003ch2>Dynamics in practical language\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A compressor turns down signal above a chosen threshold by a ratio, often with attack/release timing. A 4:1 ratio is not a fixed −4 dB adjustment; for material 8 dB above threshold, it nominally leaves roughly 2 dB above threshold (6 dB reduction) before knee, timing, makeup gain and other variables. \u003cstrong>Attack\u003c/strong> controls how quickly compression responds; \u003cstrong>release\u003c/strong> controls recovery. \u003cstrong>Noise reduction\u003c/strong> estimates or separates noise; excessive amounts often create warbling, metallic artifacts or swallowed consonants.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Build a measured dialogue-and-music example\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Use self-recorded 20–30 seconds of speech. The included WAV examples are synthetic tones/ambience, \u003cstrong>not human speech\u003c/strong>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Listen first at reasonable output volume; identify bad edits and obvious clipping.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Adjust clip gain so the voice has workable headroom; do not normalize every micro-syllable.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use subtle EQ or repair only for an identified problem; bypass to compare.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Add a music track, lower it beneath speech, and automate an intentional rise only during a gap.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use appropriate meters/loudness analysis, noting the delivery specification you are aiming for.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Export a 48 kHz audio-inclusive video; listen to the rendered file through headphones and speakers.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep a copy of unprocessed dialogue and a note of your output peak/loudness readings.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Mastery:\u003c/strong> Explain why clip gain, track volume, compressor threshold, sample peak, true peak and LUFS are not interchangeable. See L038–L045 and the symptom-oriented audio troubleshooting entries.\u003c/p>\n","description":"Field guide 02 — Audio levels, loudness, repair and delivery Read after: L038–L046. Goal: Understand the signal path, not memorize a target number. Audio as a sequence of stages Audio arrives as samples in a clip, then p"},{"id":"03-COLOR-PIPELINE","title":"A reliable color pipeline from camera signal to delivered file","filename":"guides/03-COLOR-PIPELINE.md","html":"\u003ch1>Field guide 03 — A reliable color pipeline from camera signal to delivered file\u003c/h1>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Read after:\u003c/strong> L055–L061. \u003cstrong>Goal:\u003c/strong> Distinguish technical interpretation from creative appearance.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>The four separate decisions\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Identify camera input.\u003c/strong> Determine source color gamut, transfer function, bit depth and whether media is standard video, Log or RAW. The filename extension alone is not enough.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Choose working/output intent.\u003c/strong> Establish SDR Rec.709 or a justified HDR specification early, and confirm the actual sequence color-management settings in this Premiere version.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Normalize the input.\u003c/strong> Apply the correct supported input interpretation/transform where necessary, avoiding a second transform to already normalized media.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Grade creatively and deliver.\u003c/strong> Balance white point, exposure, contrast and skin tones across shots, then export with coherent target metadata; view the actual output under controlled conditions.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>A camera Log encoding is deliberately flat looking when viewed without its intended transform. Applying random creative LUTs until the image “pops” is not equivalent to choosing the correct camera input transform. A \u003cstrong>technical LUT/transform\u003c/strong> maps signals into a working/display space. A \u003cstrong>creative LUT\u003c/strong> changes appearance. Creative grading may happen before or after specific transforms depending on the intentional pipeline; the critical point is to know what signals the operation expects.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>SDR and HDR essentials\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Rec.709\u003c/strong> describes a widely used HDTV color system, but real output appearance also depends on transfer functions, display calibration, operating-system display management and video-player interpretation. “HDR” is not just brighter SDR: an HDR pipeline involves appropriate source dynamic range, working/output transfer functions, bit depth, compatible monitors and metadata. Do not assume a normal 8-bit monitor accurately previews an HDR grade.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Scopes are measurement tools, not taste meters.\u003c/strong> A waveform helps compare luma distribution, RGB parade helps identify channel imbalances, vectorscope helps evaluate hue/saturation tendencies. None guarantees a perfectly matching image without lighting, viewing conditions and content judgment. Skin tones occupy a family of hues, not a magic line to which every complexion should be forced.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Why exported color can differ\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Before blaming an encoder, check these variables separately:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Source interpretation: was Log or RAW recognized and transformed correctly?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sequence working color space and tone mapping: is the sequence SDR while footage was HDR?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Export settings: what color tag, bit depth and codec were actually written?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>GPU/display monitoring: was the display calibrated and was color management enabled?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Player differences: is an external viewer applying its own transfer conversion or metadata interpretation?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Comparison method: are you comparing the same frame at the same brightness and display configuration?\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Do a \u003cstrong>short 10-second test export\u003c/strong> with a neutral gray/skin reference and two difficult highlights. Reimport it into Premiere, check scopes/metadata, compare in another managed application, and write down the settings. Reimport comparison is informative but not proof that every external viewer will match.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Color correction drill\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Pick a 15-second cut containing three camera angles; include a neutral wall/object.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Inspect footage interpretation and sequence color options before touching creative sliders.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Normalize each input as appropriate for its camera specification.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Correct white balance and exposure first; compare scopes and visual continuity at cuts.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Match contrast and saturation conservatively; look for clipped highlights and crushed shadows.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Add a gentle creative look, then toggle the look off to verify the underlying corrections are still sound.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Export a short SDR review, noting codec, color settings, display conditions and any player discrepancy.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Mastery:\u003c/strong> Explain why a wrong input transform, creative LUT, export color tag and uncalibrated viewer are four different potential causes of a mismatch. See L055–L061 and the export guide.\u003c/p>\n","description":"Field guide 03 — A reliable color pipeline from camera signal to delivered file Read after: L055–L061. Goal: Distinguish technical interpretation from creative appearance. The four separate decisions 1. Identify camera i"},{"id":"04-EXPORT-LAB","title":"Export lab: codecs, containers, masters and quality control","filename":"guides/04-EXPORT-LAB.md","html":"\u003ch1>Field guide 04 — Export lab: codecs, containers, masters and quality control\u003c/h1>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Read after:\u003c/strong> L083–L090. \u003cstrong>Goal:\u003c/strong> Deliver the right file for the intended viewer, not merely click Export.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Separate five independent properties\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>sequence\u003c/strong> is an editing timebase and a list of instructions. A \u003cstrong>preview render\u003c/strong> stores temporary rendered frames that may speed playback; it is not automatically the final delivered file. An \u003cstrong>export\u003c/strong> constructs a new media file. A \u003cstrong>container\u003c/strong> (for example MP4, MOV or MXF) organizes tracks and metadata. A \u003cstrong>codec\u003c/strong> (for example H.264, HEVC, ProRes or DNxHR) defines the stream's representation. A codec may be supported in several containers, and a container does not guarantee a codec.\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>Intended use\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Typical starting point\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Tradeoff to verify\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Everyday web review\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>H.264 in MP4, appropriate audio\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Broad compatibility and moderate size; compression artifacts at low rates\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Smaller high-efficiency delivery\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>HEVC/H.265 if clients support it\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>May decode more slowly; hardware/bit-depth/rights support varies\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>High-quality interchange/master\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>A supported intraframe ProRes or DNxHR profile in appropriate wrapper\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Large files; receiver must support chosen profile and exact alpha/color needs\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Graphics with transparency\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>An alpha-capable codec or image sequence, with correctly configured alpha export\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>H.264 MP4 is not a general-purpose alpha delivery choice\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Image sequence or VFX handoff\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>PNG/EXR or other supported image frames as specified by pipeline\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Many files; frame numbering, color space and audio synchronization matter\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Broadcast handoff\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Exact broadcaster spec for wrapper, codec, channels, loudness and color\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Never guess the requirement from “broadcast-quality”\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\u003ch2>Bitrate and size without fake precision\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For a simple estimate, file size in bytes is approximately\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>(video bitrate in bits/s + audio bitrate in bits/s) × seconds / 8\u003c/code>,\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>plus container overhead. For a \u003cstrong>60-second\u003c/strong> file with average 12 Mb/s video and 0.192 Mb/s audio: \u003ccode>(12,000,000 + 192,000) × 60 / 8 ≈ 91.4 MB\u003c/code>. VBR can move around an average/target, and the actual file can differ. A high bitrate does not recreate missing source detail; lower bitrate can be enough for low-motion/simple video but not a hard grainy concert.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>CBR\u003c/strong> targets a steady data rate (depending on encoder implementation). \u003cstrong>VBR\u003c/strong> allocates more bits to complex moments and fewer to simple ones; quality and size depend on rate control mode and encoder. One-pass versus multi-pass, codec profile, reference frames, GOP length, hardware encoding, bit depth and motion complexity all matter. Hardware encoding can be fast but compatibility and visual results vary by card, codec, profile and driver.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>A repeatable deliverable process\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Confirm required dimensions, frame rate, scan type, color space, codec, container, audio channels, loudness, maximum size and naming standard. For social destinations, confirm the \u003cem>current\u003c/em> platform spec independently if the file is being delivered later than the guide date.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Review sequence settings and source frame rates; avoid accidental frame-rate conversions.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Save a milestone copy, remove disabled scratch tracks from export consideration, and set intended export range.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose a preset as a starting point; inspect every parameter against the brief rather than trusting the label.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Export a short 10–20-second difficult section first, with high motion, dialogue, titles and a fade if those are present.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Watch \u003cstrong>the exported file\u003c/strong>, not just the Program Monitor. Scrub first/middle/last frames and inspect black frames, audio dropouts, typo/caption position and color.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Inspect file properties (actual resolution/frame rate, codec profile, duration, audio sample rate, channels and bitrate). Check loudness with a suitable measurement tool when required.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deliver the compressed viewing copy separately from the archival master; retain original source media and licenses.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003ch2>Failure isolation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Export fails at one timecode: replace or temporarily disable one effect/transition in a \u003cstrong>duplicate\u003c/strong> sequence; check media corruption and plugins.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Export is soft: compare source resolution, scaling algorithm, encoder bitrate and whether proxies/previews entered the export path.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Video stutters but audio continues: check variable-frame-rate source, frame-rate mismatch or player decode capacity.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Audio missing: inspect routing, track mute/solo, output channel mapping and whether the export includes audio.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Color washed out: work through the four color pipeline decisions in field guide 03.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>File too large: compute bitrate budget first; do not solve size only by shrinking resolution if that breaks delivery requirements.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Mastery:\u003c/strong> Produce a documented web review MP4 and a separately specified high-quality master, verify both in an external player, and explain the tradeoff in your own words.\u003c/p>\n","description":"Field guide 04 — Export lab: codecs, containers, masters and quality control Read after: L083–L090. Goal: Deliver the right file for the intended viewer, not merely click Export. Separate five independent properties A se"},{"id":"05-WINDOWS-VM","title":"Premiere in a Windows virtual machine, manual on Linux","filename":"guides/05-WINDOWS-VM.md","html":"\u003ch1>Field guide 05 — Premiere in a Windows virtual machine, manual on Linux\u003c/h1>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Read after:\u003c/strong> L005, L014, L097. \u003cstrong>Goal:\u003c/strong> Know what virtualization does and does not promise.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>This manual is static local HTML, CSS and JavaScript. You can extract it and read it in an ordinary Linux browser while Premiere runs separately in Windows. \u003cstrong>The editing application is Windows-only in this course\u003c/strong>; no Linux menus or Linux-only editing workflows are presented.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Adobe's Windows system requirements describe supported native Windows hardware and software. A Windows VM having a nominally supported Windows version does \u003cstrong>not\u003c/strong> prove Premiere or a GPU path inside it is supported or fast. Hypervisors, virtual graphics adapters, remote-display layers and passthrough setups vary substantially. Verify with your installed Adobe build, your hypervisor, your physical GPU and your driver combination.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>A capability checklist\u003c/h2>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>Area\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>What to verify\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>If absent or weak\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>GPU adapter\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Does the guest see a capable, supported physical GPU or only a virtual display adapter?\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Use software paths where supported; avoid GPU-heavy effects and HDR grading assumptions\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>GPU passthrough\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Is device passthrough correctly isolated, driver-installed and permitted by the host?\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Don't assume a virtual 3D adapter equals dedicated video hardware\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Hardware decode\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Does the exact source codec, chroma format, bit depth and profile decode in hardware in this guest?\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Create intraframe proxies or transcode; inspect CPU utilization and dropped frames\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Hardware encode\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Is encoder support actually exposed for the requested profile?\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Use software export where available and allow more processing time\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Memory\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Is RAM reserved for guest, host and GPU, with adequate headroom?\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Reduce concurrent apps, use proxies and smaller previews; avoid swapping\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Storage\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Are source, cache, previews and export on fast, reliably mounted volumes?\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Prefer fast local guest-accessible storage; test shared-folder throughput\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Audio\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Is a stable output device and sample rate recognized in guest?\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Check Windows audio device, Premiere hardware preferences and remote audio latency\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Color\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Is guest display pipeline accurately color-managed?\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Avoid treating remote desktop/virtual display as a grading reference\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Licensing\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Does Creative Cloud activation work in guest?\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>License, connectivity and device policies are separate from this offline manual\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\u003ch2>Conservative setup\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Confirm Premiere version and Windows guest build from their own About/System screens; record CPU/GPU/RAM/driver/hypervisor configuration.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Test a known 1080p25 sample MP4 in a \u003cstrong>new empty project\u003c/strong> before moving a real job into the VM.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Record playback smoothness with no effects, then with Lumetri and one scaled clip; note actual frame drops, not subjective “looks fast.”\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Test Media Encoder/software export and playback of its exported result; measure wall-clock export time for a 15-second sample.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>If decode is slow, make proxies from \u003cstrong>originals\u003c/strong>, enable them for editing, then verify masters reconnect correctly for final output.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep project files and source on stable paths. VM shared-folder remounts may change Windows paths; moving only the \u003ccode>.prproj\u003c/code> does not move camera originals.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Maintain a native Windows fallback when a client deadline or color-critical work makes VM uncertainties unacceptable.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003ch2>Cross-machine progress\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The website may store completion, bookmarks, notes and quiz choices in browser-local storage. \u003cstrong>That storage is not synced between Linux and Windows.\u003c/strong> Use \u003cstrong>Export progress\u003c/strong> in the top bar to save a JSON file, copy that file between computers by your usual trusted method, and use \u003cstrong>Import\u003c/strong> in the other browser. Import replaces the current browser's study record after confirmation. If a browser blocks persistence on local files, export before closing the page. None of this requires a network connection.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Mastery:\u003c/strong> Report exactly which GPU/codec/acceleration functions are \u003cem>observed\u003c/em> inside the guest, distinguish them from theoretical hardware support and demonstrate a successful archival export using original footage.\u003c/p>\n","description":"Field guide 05 — Premiere in a Windows virtual machine, manual on Linux Read after: L005, L014, L097. Goal: Know what virtualization does and does not promise. This manual is static local HTML, CSS and JavaScript. You ca"},{"id":"06-AI-SERVICE-BOUNDARIES","title":"AI-assisted editing in the verified stable release","filename":"guides/06-AI-SERVICE-BOUNDARIES.md","html":"\u003ch1>Field guide 06 — AI-assisted editing in the verified stable release\u003c/h1>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Read after:\u003c/strong> L076–L082. \u003cstrong>Goal:\u003c/strong> Use automation with clear provenance and truthful offline expectations.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>This guide uses Adobe Premiere \u003cstrong>26.5.2\u003c/strong> as the latest stable version verified on \u003cstrong>2026-10-28\u003c/strong>. The requested research date of October 29, 2026, had not yet occurred. Later updates, account-specific controls or cloud service changes may differ. The following is a \u003cstrong>capability map\u003c/strong>, not a promise that all features run in every installed seat.\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>Capability\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Stable-release status by Oct 28 research\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Important boundary\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Transcription and text-based editing\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Established desktop functionality\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Language/model support, download and connectivity may be configuration-dependent\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Paper Edit\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Added in 26.5\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Build a sequence from transcript selections; verify edits and speaker/context carefully\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Generative Media Tool\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Added in 26.5\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Adobe service, supported model, account eligibility and potentially credits required; not an offline operation\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Generative Extend\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Stable in current product, expanded availability in 26.5\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Generated frames/audio may require remote processing, supported media constraints and generative credits\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Object Masking\u003c/strong> and smoothing\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Stable, with smoothing enhancements in 26.5\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Inspect edges, occlusion, temporal drift and performance on actual footage\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Enhance Speech\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Supported Adobe audio workflow\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Quality is input-dependent; verify current processing requirements in your installed version\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Automatic Reframe, scene detection, captions\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Established assisted workflows\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Always review crop, cut points, spelling and reading speed\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003cstrong>AI Assistant\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Beta/not established as 26.5.2 stable\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Not taught as a stable required step in this course\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Premiere 27.0\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Future/upcoming as researched\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Do not attribute announced changes to stable 26.5.2\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>The exact naming and UI location of a fast-changing service can move between updates. Verify using Adobe's live current documentation \u003cstrong>when online\u003c/strong>, but this course remains usable offline for concept study. A feature being installed does not necessarily mean cloud generation is possible while offline; a connection and service entitlements can be separate constraints from application launch.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Responsible generated-media procedure\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Choose a task such as extending a shot for a transition where generated content is appropriate, rather than covering essential documentary evidence.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Work in a duplicate sequence and preserve a record of the original source extent.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Confirm whether the action calls a remote model and what service terms, limits, credit costs and copyright/client approval apply to your account.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Generate only appropriate content and compare the added frames with adjacent source, paying attention to hands, text, logos, geometry, motion and continuity.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Check audio artifacts, resolution, color changes, duration and whether the generated result is approved for the intended client context.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep source, generated results and editorial decisions distinguishable in bins, naming and delivery documentation.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003ch2>Text-assisted editing checks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A transcript helps locate candidate cuts but is not authoritative: false words, speaker confusion, overlapping dialogue, abrupt cuts and context changes occur. Verify critical claims by listening to source recordings, viewing edit boundaries and checking whether picture continuity still works. For subtitles, correct the text and timing manually and check readability, especially where one-word caption styles create high visual density.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Mastery:\u003c/strong> Identify which AI operation is available offline, requires services or remains beta; describe its failure modes; and provide a human-reviewed export rather than relying on the automated preview alone.\u003c/p>\n","description":"Field guide 06 — AI-assisted editing in the verified stable release Read after: L076–L082. Goal: Use automation with clear provenance and truthful offline expectations. This guide uses Adobe Premiere 26.5.2 as the latest"},{"id":"07-CAPSTONE-PRODUCTION","title":"Professional capstone production brief and acceptance tests","filename":"guides/07-CAPSTONE-PRODUCTION.md","html":"\u003ch1>Field guide 07 — Professional capstone production brief and acceptance tests\u003c/h1>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Read after:\u003c/strong> Stage 13; complete L096–L105 as preparation or use their briefs selectively.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>The brief\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Produce a \u003cstrong>2:30–3:30 minute\u003c/strong> documentary portrait, product story or community profile using footage and audio you personally record or have permission to use. Deliver a master, a viewing copy, a caption sidecar and a self-contained archive. An entirely self-recorded phone interview with B-roll is enough. No paid stock videos, music or additional Adobe applications are required. Synthetic sample media bundled with this course can support early technical exercises but is not a substitute for a compelling human story.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Story and shot design\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Write a one-paragraph premise and select one specific audience. Capture or source, with permission, an A-roll interview or narration, establishing shot, several contextual details, a reaction/meaningful moment and a visual conclusion. Record quiet room tone and a clean sync reference. Create a shot log including frame rate, resolution, audio sample rate, camera, date, consent and usage rights. Organize disk folders and Premiere bins so a stranger can reconstruct the project.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Production gates\u003c/h2>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>Gate\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Deliverable\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Pass/fail test\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>1. Ingest\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Verified source manifest and at least two copies of originals\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Randomly open three source files from backup\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>2. Story\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Logline, selects and rough sequence\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>An independent reviewer can identify subject and stakes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>3. Picture lock\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>2:30–3:30 export with intentional cuts and no orphan gaps\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Watch the whole cut; no accidental black frames or sync errors\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>4. Dialogue/music mix\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Clean intelligible voice, controlled music, no audible edit clicks\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Listen on headphones and small speakers; record peak/loudness reading\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>5. Color\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Consistent shot-to-shot exposure/white balance and intentional look\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Inspect at cuts and on scopes; no accidental double-transform\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>6. Titles/captions\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Legible title/lower third, synchronized and proofread caption track\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Check names, spelling, on-screen safe areas and timing\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>7. Output\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>1080p review MP4 and a separately specified high-quality master\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Open both; inspect duration, size, codec, frame rate, audio and color\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>8. Handoff\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Captions (SRT if requested), manifest, editorial notes, project, sources, licenses\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Reopen the archived project from its new location\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\u003ch2>A simple edit decision log\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For every significant editorial change write: \u003cstrong>sequence version\u003c/strong>, \u003cstrong>timecode or rough range\u003c/strong>, \u003cstrong>problem\u003c/strong>, \u003cstrong>action\u003c/strong>, \u003cstrong>why\u003c/strong>, and \u003cstrong>recheck needed\u003c/strong>. Example: “v04 / 01:17–01:24 / hand gesture jumps / insert product detail before line / preserves meaning and masks visual jump / check music keyframe afterward.” The discipline matters more than the tool used for the log.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Delivery examples, not contractual specifications\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>review copy\u003c/strong> might be 1920×1080 progressive, same nominal frame rate as the locked sequence, H.264 in MP4 with stereo audio. A \u003cstrong>high-quality master\u003c/strong> might use a supported intraframe codec such as ProRes/DNxHR where appropriate. Choose the exact profile, bit depth, color, alpha support and loudness targets from the receiver's technical brief; do not infer standards from these examples. A \u003cstrong>vertical variant\u003c/strong> can be 1080×1920, but it must be recomposed and retimed where needed rather than merely rotated.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Independent evaluation rubric (100 points)\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Organization, source integrity and relinking — \u003cstrong>15\u003c/strong>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Story, shot selection, continuity and pacing — \u003cstrong>20\u003c/strong>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Technical editing, frame accuracy and synchronization — \u003cstrong>15\u003c/strong>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Dialogue clarity, sound design and delivery listening QC — \u003cstrong>15\u003c/strong>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Color, graphics and accessibility — \u003cstrong>15\u003c/strong>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Correct output files, metadata and caption handling — \u003cstrong>10\u003c/strong>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Archive reopen, licensing notes and decision log — \u003cstrong>10\u003c/strong>.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Passing standard:\u003c/strong> 80/100 overall, with no failed source integrity, audio clipping, missing caption deliverables when requested or broken final export. Keep a small “known imperfections” report; professional work is also the ability to explain remaining risks.\u003c/p>\n","description":"Field guide 07 — Professional capstone production brief and acceptance tests Read after: Stage 13; complete L096–L105 as preparation or use their briefs selectively. The brief Produce a 2:30–3:30 minute documentary portr"},{"id":"08-TIMECODE-TECHNICAL","title":"Frame rates, drop-frame timecode and media-compatibility math","filename":"guides/08-TIMECODE-TECHNICAL.md","html":"\u003ch1>Field guide 08 — Frame rates, drop-frame timecode and media-compatibility math\u003c/h1>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Read after:\u003c/strong> L002, L013, L015 and L089. \u003cstrong>Goal:\u003c/strong> Keep timing and file formats conceptually separate.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Timecode is an address system\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Timecode \u003ccode>HH:MM:SS:FF\u003c/code> identifies a frame position using a frame-counting convention. The \u003ccode>FF\u003c/code> component denotes frames within a second, not decimal hundredths. At 25 fps, \u003ccode>00:00:03:24\u003c/code> is one frame before \u003ccode>00:00:04:00\u003c/code>. If a 25 fps clip contains 250 frames, nominal duration is 10 seconds. An edit boundary must be interpreted using the actual sequence frame rate.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>29.97 fps\u003c/strong> is shorthand for approximately \u003cstrong>30000/1001\u003c/strong> frames per second. \u003cstrong>Drop-frame timecode\u003c/strong> does not remove real video frames. It omits selected timecode \u003cem>numbers\u003c/em> to keep displayed time addresses aligned more closely with elapsed clock time. At 29.97 DF, frame labels \u003ccode>:00\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>:01\u003c/code> are skipped at the start of each minute except every tenth minute. Thus some apparently missing codes are intentional; the picture is continuous. 23.976, 29.97 and 59.94 require care with exact rational rates, particularly in long-form calculations.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Constant versus variable frame cadence\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Constant-frame-rate media uses a stable cadence. Phone screen recordings or mobile captures can have variable presentation timestamps; the nominal “30 fps” label might summarize a changing actual cadence. A variable-frame-rate recording can create sync drift, confusing timecode navigation or inconsistent decode. When problems occur, investigate a \u003cstrong>constant-frame-rate transcode\u003c/strong> that preserves intended playback duration, then relink/replace correctly and test sync. Do not randomly “interpret” frame rate to force a sync solution.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Media layers you can inspect\u003c/h2>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>Layer\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Example\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Why it matters\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Container\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>MP4\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Stores encoded streams and timing/metadata\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Video codec\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>H.264/AVC\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Dictates decoding, hardware support, bitrate and compression characteristics\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Pixel format\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>4:2:0, 10-bit\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Affects chroma detail, precision and GPU support\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Color metadata\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Rec.709 or Log/transfer tag\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Helps define signal interpretation; may be incomplete/wrong\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Audio codec\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>AAC or PCM\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Affects file size, compatibility and processing loss\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Sample rate\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>48,000 samples/s\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Affects audio-clock relationship and delivery convention\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>GOP structure\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>All-intra vs long-GOP\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Affects seek, edit responsiveness and file size\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>A high-quality 4:2:2 10-bit HEVC file can be harder for a particular GPU to decode than a larger intraframe proxy. Hardware support is a matrix of exact profile/bit-depth/chroma/driver/device; “my GPU supports HEVC” is not enough detail.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>One technical worksheet\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Given a 25 fps sequence containing 500 frames of picture, one 48 kHz stereo PCM audio stream and a planned H.264 delivery:\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Picture duration: \u003ccode>500 / 25 = 20 seconds\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Expected samples per audio channel in a perfect 20-second output: \u003ccode>48,000 × 20 = 960,000\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A hypothetical 8 Mb/s average video stream for 20 seconds: \u003ccode>8,000,000 × 20 / 8 = 20,000,000 bytes\u003c/code> (before audio and overhead).\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Actual final file size differs due to the audio codec, encoder rate control and wrapper overhead.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Check the \u003cstrong>rendered export\u003c/strong> for real duration, audio channel assignment and black/missing frames.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Mastery:\u003c/strong> Distinguish physical frame loss from drop-frame \u003cem>numbering\u003c/em>, nominal rates from exact rational frame rates, and a wrapper extension from the codec and color signal inside it.\u003c/p>\n","description":"Field guide 08 — Frame rates, drop-frame timecode and media-compatibility math Read after: L002, L013, L015 and L089. Goal: Keep timing and file formats conceptually separate. Timecode is an address system Timecode HH:MM"},{"id":"09-ACCESSIBILITY-CAPTIONS","title":"Titles, captions, transcripts and accessible review","filename":"guides/09-ACCESSIBILITY-CAPTIONS.md","html":"\u003ch1>Field guide 09 — Titles, captions, transcripts and accessible review\u003c/h1>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Read after:\u003c/strong> L062–L068. \u003cstrong>Goal:\u003c/strong> Know which text artifact your audience and client actually needs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>title/graphic\u003c/strong> is visual design inside the picture. A \u003cstrong>transcript\u003c/strong> is a text representation of spoken language, usually with timestamps or speaker information; it can support Paper Edit and text-based editing. A \u003cstrong>caption track\u003c/strong> contains timed text intended to be read alongside audio. \u003cstrong>Open/burned-in captions\u003c/strong> are permanently visible in image pixels; \u003cstrong>closed/selectable captions or subtitle files\u003c/strong> can often be toggled in compatible players or platforms. The precise terminology and support depend on the delivery format and player.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Practical distinctions\u003c/h2>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>Requirement\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Appropriate starting workflow\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Necessary check\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Names and lower thirds\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Text/graphics and current Properties panel\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Safe area, contrast, spelling, timing and brand consistency\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Readable speech captions\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Transcript review → timed caption segments\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Accuracy, speaker attribution, line breaks and reading rate\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Selectable subtitle sidecar\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Export requested SRT or compatible sidecar format\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Frame rate/timebase, encoding, exact beginning/ending and platform ingestion\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Burned-in visual captions\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Render captions into the video only when specified\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>They cannot be disabled, and visual placement affects reframe\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Localization\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Separately proofread translations and timings\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Do not mistake machine translation for human language review\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>In current Premiere documentation, many title-editing controls live in the \u003cstrong>Properties\u003c/strong> panel, not in the historical Essential Graphics Edit panel shown in old videos. Check the installed build rather than applying outdated UI steps from screenshots.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Accessibility is practical\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use sufficient luminance contrast over moving backgrounds; a short drop shadow or unobtrusive backing shape can help. Avoid forcing a viewer to read large blocks while a complex visual action occurs. Keep text inside the delivery's safe composition area, including vertical reframes and smartphone UI overlays. Identify speakers when needed, and represent meaningful non-speech sounds if the caption standard calls for it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Automatic transcript/caption tools are draft assistants, not final proofreaders. Common errors include names, specialized terms, numerals, homophones, speaker overlaps, sound effects and phrase boundaries. A one-word-per-caption creative style may be visually trendy but can harm readability in some contexts. Client specification and audience take priority over the style preset.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Repeatable exercise\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Record 30 seconds of narration yourself or obtain consented speech.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Generate a transcript using a supported workflow. If unavailable offline, transcribe manually.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Correct every word by listening to the original, paying attention to names and punctuation.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Create caption segments with intentional phrase grouping and timing; watch playback at 1×.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Design one lower third using the Properties panel, checking margins in a 16:9 and 9:16 mockup.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Export a caption sidecar and an optional caption-burned review. Confirm which is actually required by the brief.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reopen the SRT in a plain text editor and check first/last cues, UTF-8 characters and a random middle cue.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Mastery:\u003c/strong> Explain the differences among graphics, transcripts, captions, SRT sidecars, and burned-in text; demonstrate a proofread accessible delivery instead of trusting automated text blindly.\u003c/p>\n","description":"Field guide 09 — Titles, captions, transcripts and accessible review Read after: L062–L068. Goal: Know which text artifact your audience and client actually needs. A title/graphic is visual design inside the picture. A t"},{"id":"10-EFFECT-LIBRARY-AUDIT","title":"Premiere 26.x effects, transitions and backward compatibility","filename":"guides/10-EFFECT-LIBRARY-AUDIT.md","html":"\u003ch1>Field guide 10 — Premiere 26.x effects, transitions and backward compatibility\u003c/h1>\n\u003ch2>Why old effect tutorials break\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Premiere 26.0 reorganized the Effects panel. Adobe published four materially different categories: \u003cstrong>active current effects\u003c/strong>, \u003cstrong>Legacy\u003c/strong>, \u003cstrong>Obsolete/hidden for new use\u003c/strong>, and \u003cstrong>Removed (will not render)\u003c/strong>. Later 26.2–26.5 releases introduced modern replacements with names that sometimes match the removed ones. An effect \u003cstrong>name alone\u003c/strong> therefore cannot establish which plug-in is being demonstrated. This is a technical compatibility issue rather than a cosmetic panel rearrangement.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>This guide is specific to Premiere 26.5.2 for Windows, as researched October 28, 2026. See the Effects and Transitions CSVs for the individually classified items, and check the installed Effects panel for patch-specific controls.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>A four-way decision tree\u003c/h2>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>If an older tutorial says…\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>First-pass assumption to reject\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Version-aware approach\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Apply \u003cstrong>Crop\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>It is necessarily the current crop effect\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Adobe 26.0 lists Crop under Legacy. Try modern Rounded Crop if it provides the desired geometry, and verify alpha edges.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Apply \u003cstrong>Magnify\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>The old and new plug-ins are identical\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Adobe introduced the modern Magnify in 26.3; the older Magnify is Legacy. Recreate the controls, do not paste legacy parameter values blindly.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Apply \u003cstrong>Lens Flare\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>It was continuously available with identical output\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>The old plug-in was Obsolete in 26.0; a new adjustable Lens Flare arrived in 26.5. Compare source versions.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Apply \u003cstrong>Noise\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>The old Noise remains the same\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Older Noise was removed in 26.0; a modern Noise effect arrived in 26.2.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Apply \u003cstrong>Channel Blur\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Any old Channel Blur preset will open unchanged\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Old Channel Blur was removed in 26.0; modern Channel Blur has a different implementation and more color-mode support.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Apply \u003cstrong>Twirl\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>An old Twirl tutorial's parameters are current\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>26.5 rebuilt Twirl; the old version moved under Legacy.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Apply \u003cstrong>Cross Dissolve\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>It is the same engine used in an old project\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Adobe lists modern Cross Dissolve and separate Cross Dissolve (Legacy). Compare blend, preview, GPU and handle requirements.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Use \u003cstrong>Gradient Wipe\u003c/strong> as video transition\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>An older transition name implies current availability\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>26.0 documents Gradient Wipe among removed video transitions. Do not teach it as a default active transition.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>Note: Adobe's 26.0 inventory can list a historical plug-in as removed and also list a replacement of the \u003cstrong>same displayed name\u003c/strong> as active. This is not a contradiction: the identities and processing engines differ.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Built-in versus optional\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Adobe's modern effects and transitions are included with current Premiere; the official modern-effects documentation describes Film Impact-derived effects as bundled, not separately purchased add-ons. \u003cstrong>This does not mean every commercial Film Impact effect or preset pack is in Premiere.\u003c/strong> Never assume an effect exists solely because an online tutorial uses an “FX” or “Impacts” suffix. The documentation uses historical promotional suffixes and newer Effects-panel names; confirm the current label in the target UI.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A third-party OFX/VST/video plug-in, an After Effects effect and a native Premiere effect are different dependencies. For archival delivery, document every external dependency and render a high-quality intermediate for effects that cannot be reproduced on the receiver's system.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Exercise: rebuild a color and blur stack\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Create a copy of the practice sequence containing \u003ccode>SHOT_A_motion.mp4\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>SHOT_B_colorbars.mp4\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>On the first clip, apply \u003cstrong>Lumetri Color\u003c/strong> and make a subtle exposure change. Compare scopes before/after.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Apply the current \u003cstrong>Gaussian Blur\u003c/strong> with a small radius. Toggle both effects separately in Effect Controls, and observe whether order affects the result.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Repeat on a duplicate with \u003cstrong>Channel Blur\u003c/strong>. Check whether it offers independent channels and how those differ from whole-image blur.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Inspect any separate Legacy item only on a duplicate. Do not assume parameter parity.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Export a five-second inspection MP4 and compare it against the timeline preview at 100% scale.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Acceptance:\u003c/strong> you can identify which plug-in version was used, the controls changed, preview differences, and whether the received export matches your intention.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Transition handle laboratory\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cut a 5-second A shot into a 5-second B shot. With no trimmed handles, a centered dissolve may have insufficient additional frames; Premiere may shorten the transition, offset alignment or repeat frames. Trim 12 frames from the end of A and 12 from the start of B and try again. Compare a direct cut, Cross Dissolve and Dip to Black. Describe why a fade can conceal continuity or create an unintended emotional pause.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Distinguish \u003cstrong>transition length in sequence frames\u003c/strong> from \u003cstrong>source handles\u003c/strong>. A 25 fps sequence's 25-frame transition lasts one second; it is not 25 additional source frames on every side by default.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Handling legacy projects safely\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Duplicate the project and preserve an unopened source archive.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Open in a documented version and inspect every Effects Control stack for missing/obsolete items.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>For each issue, record the clip, media time, specific old plug-in identity and expected picture.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Replace using an explicitly mapped modern equivalent only when a visual match is possible; a similar name does not guarantee a match.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Export a before/after range; preserve both copies for review.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Do not discard the older software/environment when exact legacy rendering is contractual and the original effect is removed.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003ch2>Source and verification scope\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Adobe's \u003ca href=\"https://helpx.adobe.com/premiere/desktop/add-video-effects/effects-and-transitions-library/list-of-effects-and-transitions.html\">26.0 effect/transition changes\u003c/a> gives current, Legacy, Obsolete and Removed categories.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Adobe's \u003ca href=\"https://helpx.adobe.com/premiere/desktop/add-video-effects/types-of-effects/effects.html\">modern effects\u003c/a> describes modern replacements.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Adobe's \u003ca href=\"https://helpx.adobe.com/premiere/desktop/whats-new/release-notes.html\">September 26.5 release notes\u003c/a> document Twirl, Lens Flare and Mosaic updates.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Confidence limit:\u003c/strong> this research verifies published Adobe names and migration notes. It does not constitute an installed 26.5.2 inventory or prove every effect parameter on a Windows seat.\u003c/p>\n","description":"Field guide 10 — Premiere 26.x effects, transitions and backward compatibility Why old effect tutorials break Premiere 26.0 reorganized the Effects panel. Adobe published four materially different categories: active curr"},{"id":"11-WINDOWS-SHORTCUT-LAB","title":"Windows shortcut mastery and conflict audit","filename":"guides/11-WINDOWS-SHORTCUT-LAB.md","html":"\u003ch1>Field guide 11 — Windows shortcut mastery and conflict audit\u003c/h1>\n\u003ch2>Do not memorize a key without its context\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Premiere keyboard behavior depends on \u003cstrong>the current active panel\u003c/strong>, the \u003cstrong>shortcut set\u003c/strong>, and sometimes the \u003cstrong>selection and track targeting\u003c/strong>. The same binding may perform different actions in separate panels. For example, \u003ccode>Ctrl+L\u003c/code> is Link for selected clips in the editing context, but Adobe lists \u003ccode>Ctrl+L\u003c/code> as Loop in the Audio Track Mixer. \u003ccode>Ctrl+R\u003c/code> opens Speed/Duration for the relevant clip but controls Show Rulers in the Program Monitor. Both appear in Adobe's documented Windows tables. The manual marks panel-specific actions explicitly.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>First check \u003cstrong>Edit &gt; Keyboard Shortcuts\u003c/strong>. Inspect \u003cstrong>Set\u003c/strong>, the Application versus selected Panel commands, the visible key assignments, and any conflict warnings. Never overwrite a working custom preset just to follow a tutorial. Save a named copy first.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>A practical, staged keyboard curriculum\u003c/h2>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>Week\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Drill\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Reliable documented Windows shortcuts\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Success measurement\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>1\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Save and open\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>Ctrl+S\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Ctrl+O\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Ctrl+I\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Ctrl+M\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Open, import, save and enter Export without hunting menus.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>1\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Navigation and review\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>I\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>O\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Shift+I\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Shift+O\u003c/code>; playback \u003ccode>J\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>K\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>L\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Space\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Select exactly 20 source frames and shuttle accurately.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>2\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Insert and overwrite\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>,\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>.\u003c/code> with \u003cstrong>source patching\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Predict whether sequence length changes before each edit.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>2\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Timeline corrections\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>Ctrl+K\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Ctrl+Shift+K\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Shift+Delete\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Alt+Backspace\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Describe targeted/all-track behavior and any ripple shift.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>3\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Clip handling\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>F\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>G\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Ctrl+R\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Ctrl+L\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Match source, adjust gain, speed and linked selection in intended panel.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>3\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Transitions\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>Ctrl+D\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Ctrl+Shift+D\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Shift+D\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Apply to a well-selected cut with adequate source handles.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>4\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Precision\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>Alt+Left/Right\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Ctrl+Alt+Left/Right\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Alt+,\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>Alt+.\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Distinguish nudge, slip and slide by inspecting neighboring cuts.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>4\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Organization\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>Ctrl+B\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Shift+1\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Shift+2\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Shift+3\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Shift+4\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Navigate Project, Source, Timeline and Program intentionally.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\u003ch3>Experimental check: track targeting is not source patching\u003c/h3>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Start a sequence with V1 footage and A1 audio; park the playhead in the middle.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Set Source Monitor In/Out on a new clip.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Change \u003cem>source patching\u003c/em> for V/A and insert with \u003ccode>,\u003c/code>. Record the actual receiving sequence tracks.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Undo; now change \u003cem>track targeting\u003c/em> instead and repeat while preserving patch destinations. Observe the distinction.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Enable/disable targeted tracks and invoke \u003ccode>Ctrl+K\u003c/code> on a copy. Compare with \u003ccode>Ctrl+Shift+K\u003c/code> for all participating tracks; protect anything important with locked tracks.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Pass:\u003c/strong> you can state which specific track participates in each command without trial and error.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch3>Editing geometry drill\u003c/h3>\n\u003cp>With a three-clip A–B–C sequence and handles on every clip, make four practice copies:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Ripple trim A's Out ten frames earlier: duration decreases ten frames and following material moves left if sync controls permit.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Roll A/B boundary ten frames later: A gains ten visible frames and B loses ten; total sequence duration stays unchanged.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Slip B ten source frames later: B remains in its sequence slot and C stays fixed; B's displayed source range changes.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Slide B ten sequence frames later: B's source selection/duration stay fixed; A and C compensate, subject to available handles.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Annotate the source In/Out, sequence In/Out and total sequence duration in each copy. Use keyboard shortcuts only after explaining the operation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Verified table versus other documentation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>default-keyboard-shortcuts Adobe page\u003c/strong> lists menu and panel shortcuts. \u003cstrong>Adobe's playback articles\u003c/strong> separately confirm J-K-L and Space transport, so those bindings have a different documented source. If a shortcut is not shown or behaves differently, use the installed app's \u003ccode>Edit &gt; Keyboard Shortcuts\u003c/code> before assuming the manual or the application is malfunctioning. Many commands have no default binding, especially specialized automation commands.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Key-collision worksheet\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For each binding, record: shortcut, panel, selected object, active tool, relevant track targets, resulting command, and shortcut-set name. At a minimum, test \u003ccode>Ctrl+L\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Ctrl+R\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Shift+O\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Ctrl+Shift+I\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Space\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>S\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Delete\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>Backspace\u003c/code> across their documented applicable panels. Do not conclude that a collision is an error if the panels differ.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Source and limits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ca href=\"https://helpx.adobe.com/premiere/desktop/get-started/keyboard-shortcuts/default-keyboard-shortcuts.html\">Adobe 2026 Windows default keyboard table\u003c/a>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ca href=\"https://helpx.adobe.com/premiere/desktop/get-started/keyboard-shortcuts/customize-keyboard-shortcuts.html\">Adobe keyboard customization\u003c/a>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ca href=\"https://helpx.adobe.com/premiere/desktop/render-and-export/render-sequences-for-playback/play-active-sequence-in-program-monitor.html\">Adobe Program Monitor playback and J-K-L\u003c/a>\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Confidence limit:\u003c/strong> published default rows were checked against their stated source and contexts; no live Premiere installation was used to prove the active assignment of every key. That final check depends on the installed shortcut set.\u003c/p>\n","description":"Field guide 11 — Windows shortcut mastery and conflict audit Do not memorize a key without its context Premiere keyboard behavior depends on the current active panel, the shortcut set, and sometimes the selection and tra"},{"id":"12-PRODUCTION-PIPELINE-QC","title":"End-to-end professional edit, compliance and QC pipeline","filename":"guides/12-PRODUCTION-PIPELINE-QC.md","html":"\u003ch1>Field guide 12 — End-to-end professional edit, compliance and QC pipeline\u003c/h1>\n\u003ch2>Define delivery before production\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A professional workflow begins with a \u003cstrong>delivery specification\u003c/strong>, not with a codec preset. Ask the receiver for picture dimensions, exact frame rate, color space/transfer, desired bit depth/profile, audio channel order/sample rate, loudness or true-peak thresholds, subtitle format, aspect versions, maximum file size, deadline and permitted media rights. If the receiver provides only a social-platform name, treat presets as starting points rather than a formal contract.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Stage gates\u003c/h2>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>Gate\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Check\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Proof\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>1. Intake\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Source files copied safely, rights recorded, codec/fps/audio formats inventoried\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Source manifest and backup count\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>2. Project structure\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Stable Windows paths, bins, autosave and scratch plan\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Reopen project without relinking\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>3. Assembly\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Narrative beginning/middle/end, selected takes, stable source references\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Rough-cut review file\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>4. Picture lock\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>J/L cuts, continuity, handles, approved story and exact duration\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Locked edit version and change log\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>5. Sound finish\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Noise repair is mild; clip gain before track mix; meters verify delivery\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Integrated loudness report and listening log\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>6. Image finish\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Input interpretation and sequence management verified; scopes and shot match\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Color QC stills and reference monitor notes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>7. Text finish\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Graphics safe, spelling correct, caption timing accurate\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Subtitle proofreading pass\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>8. Export\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Correct container/codec/frame rate/pixel aspect/caption mode\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Independently played encoded files and metadata\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>9. Handoff\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Receiver can extract, relink and open project; asset rights included\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Archive restoration test\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\u003ch2>High-risk distinctions to explain before working\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Sequence versus export.\u003c/strong> A 1080p sequence can be exported at 4K, but upscaling cannot recover native detail. A 4K sequence can be exported at 1080p while requiring appropriate downscaling. Sequence settings and export settings are independent decisions.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Codec versus container.\u003c/strong> H.264/H.265 are codecs; MP4/MOV are containers. A MOV is not inherently archival; it depends on the stream codec, profile, bit depth, color metadata, audio and long-term decoding environment.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Proxies versus masters.\u003c/strong> Proxies speed interactive editing. Confirm online originals before making the approved delivery, and avoid exporting lower-resolution proxy imagery accidentally.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Loudness versus peak.\u003c/strong> Integrated LUFS approximates overall perceived loudness; sample peak dBFS and true peak measure different risks. Do not satisfy one by ignoring the others. Never invent a universal streaming loudness requirement—specifications vary by receiver.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Color.\u003c/strong> A Log camera clip must be interpreted against its actual camera transfer/gamut. A display-referred creative LUT is not a reliable substitute for an input transform. After export, compare on known-managed viewing paths and document display differences.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Captions.\u003c/strong> “Burn-in” is drawn into video pixels; a sidecar such as SRT supplies text/timing but generally cannot reproduce rich styling. Embedding into a video container is format/profile constrained; verify the specific receiver/player.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Five failure-injection tests\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Offline media:\u003c/strong> rename a copy of a media directory and verify relinking; restore original paths afterward.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Unsupported decoder:\u003c/strong> transcode a duplicate of problematic compressed footage to a supported editing codec and compare behavior while preserving original source.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Broken color:\u003c/strong> deliberately assign incorrect input color settings in a test copy; compare scopes and identify the cause rather than compensating with saturation alone.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Caption mismatch:\u003c/strong> export SRT and burn-in as separate controlled tests; view the SRT in a media player to understand style loss.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Damaged handoff:\u003c/strong> move the archive to a new directory and reopen the project. If source references fail, the package is not yet finished.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003ch2>Receipt-quality handoff manifest\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Record: delivery ID; Windows Premiere patch; project file version; sequence names; original folder layout; count of media files; proxy/media dependencies; footage/music/fonts/LUT licenses; required plug-ins; export format and encoder; dimensions; frame rate; color primaries/transfer; bit depth; audio channels, sample rate, integrated loudness/peak values; caption file(s); checksums where required; reviewer/date; and any exception.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Final acceptance:\u003c/strong> all deliverables open in independent playback, start/end are correct, speech is intelligible, music isn't accidentally clipped, captions match text and timing, picture contains no unintended black frames or bad masks, and the project can be recovered from the delivered archive.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Using the included practice media\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The bundled samples are original, generated media for technical exercises. They cannot substitute for filmed conversations, emotional performance or complex production footage. For narrative capstones, record your own authorized interview/action shots; do not import copyrighted commercial videos or music without permission.\u003c/p>\n","description":"Field guide 12 — End-to-end professional edit, compliance and QC pipeline Define delivery before production A professional workflow begins with a delivery specification, not with a codec preset. Ask the receiver for pict"},{"id":"13-RESEARCH-VERIFICATION-PROTOCOL","title":"Version verification, technical uncertainty and reproducible testing","filename":"guides/13-RESEARCH-VERIFICATION-PROTOCOL.md","html":"\u003ch1>Field guide 13 — Version verification, technical uncertainty and reproducible testing\u003c/h1>\n\u003ch2>What a passing test does and does not establish\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An automated lesson-ID check proves that references resolve. A browser DOM test proves that \u003cstrong>tested controls operated in that browser test context\u003c/strong>. Neither proves that a 26.5.2 feature exists in an installed Windows copy. A dated Adobe release note proves that Adobe documented a capability for the release; it does not prove account eligibility, credit pricing, source-format compatibility or GPU behavior on a specific Windows VM.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Use four confidence levels:\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Adobe-published current:\u003c/strong> release note or current Adobe help identifies the feature/name/command and release or category.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Adobe-published historical/Legacy/Obsolete/Removed:\u003c/strong> archival or migration reference; should not be taught as an ordinary current effect.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>In-app reproducible:\u003c/strong> exact Windows build and keyboard preset tested, with before/after media and a recorded procedure. \u003cstrong>Not achieved by this manual's author in this environment.\u003c/strong>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Open hypothesis:\u003c/strong> incomplete current catalog support, unsupported hardware profile, external account or plugin-dependent result; require further evidence.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003ch2>Patch-specific revalidation checklist\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Record the installed version via Help &gt; About Premiere (or the equivalent Help &gt; About dialog visible in your seat), then compare:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Release notes and hotfixes since 26.5.2.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The available named video effects, audio effects and transitions within the Effects panel, including whether a row says Legacy.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>Edit &gt; Keyboard Shortcuts\u003c/code> command name, default key set and active context. If a key is user-customized, report that difference rather than changing the whole course.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Properties, Text, Effect Controls, Export, Color and Audio panel locations.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The complete media path/proxy path and the specific hardware decoding/encoding profiles used in the project.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cloud/AI feature's service status, account, entitlement, model and credit prompt before using it.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Captions: transcript features, editable text, export burn-in/sidecar/embedding options.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Installed plug-ins, font availability, LUT dependencies and hardware drivers.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2>How to report a suspected manual error\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Record the lesson/row ID, the exact statement, Adobe version and Windows build, current screenshot or menu label, chosen workspace/keyboard preset, precise reproduction steps, actual versus expected output and a current official source URL if possible. Mark the claim as \u003cstrong>correct\u003c/strong>, \u003cstrong>context-sensitive\u003c/strong>, \u003cstrong>changed after publication\u003c/strong>, \u003cstrong>historical only\u003c/strong>, or \u003cstrong>unverified\u003c/strong>. Avoid untraceable claims such as “Premiere always does this.”\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Explicitly untested here\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>This package was tested using published documentation, data integrity scripts, media probes and headless Chromium DOM interactions. The automation environment blocked \u003ccode>file://\u003c/code> top-level navigation. No unrestricted Linux/Windows file-origin browser run, native Windows keyboard-preset inspection, real Premiere export or Windows VM GPU passthrough test was available. These are residual deployment gates, not success claims.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Source priorities\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Adobe \u003ca href=\"https://helpx.adobe.com/premiere/desktop/whats-new/release-notes.html\">release notes\u003c/a> to establish stable version and chronology.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Adobe \u003ca href=\"https://helpx.adobe.com/premiere/desktop/get-started/keyboard-shortcuts/default-keyboard-shortcuts.html\">default shortcuts\u003c/a> for documented Windows keys.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Adobe \u003ca href=\"https://helpx.adobe.com/premiere/desktop/add-video-effects/effects-and-transitions-library/list-of-effects-and-transitions.html\">26.0 effect migration inventory\u003c/a> plus \u003ca href=\"https://helpx.adobe.com/premiere/desktop/add-video-effects/types-of-effects/effects.html\">modern effects\u003c/a> for currently named replacements.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Adobe \u003ca href=\"https://helpx.adobe.com/premiere/desktop/get-started/technical-requirements/adobe-premiere-pro-technical-requirements.html\">technical requirements\u003c/a> and media/codec documentation for support boundaries.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Real Windows installation and asset-specific test for final deployment validation.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>A good manual explicitly distinguishes \u003cstrong>what was researched\u003c/strong>, \u003cstrong>what was independently tested\u003c/strong>, and \u003cstrong>what a learner must verify on their seat\u003c/strong>.\u003c/p>\n","description":"Field guide 13 — Version verification, technical uncertainty and reproducible testing What a passing test does and does not establish An automated lesson-ID check proves that references resolve. A browser DOM test proves"}];
